  "slug": "brain-gpc-b1f5",
  "summary": "Recent preprints couple sleep-like plasticity, Hodgkin-Huxley dynamics, and foundation-model audits to test biological realism in learning systems.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is the use of biologically grounded mechanisms to explain or equip artificial neural networks. A multilayer spiking network implements unsupervised sleep-like intra- and inter-layer plasticity, reporting categorization, memory consolidation, and ATP-estimated energy efficiency. BrainFuse offers a differentiable infrastructure that couples Hodgkin-Huxley neuron dynamics with gradient-based learning and neuromorphic components. A separate analysis of a neural foundation model applies manifold analysis and a tubularity metric to assess whether its internal processing stages are biologically plausible. What is new is the joint treatment of biophysical realism, learning rules, and plausibility auditing within machine learning frameworks. The thread is dominant rather than uniform: the works differ in scale and method, and only some build models while another audits an existing one.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-01-30"
}
